      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226477/assets/d3d381842539a44e53d77697b18f0cb51552996c885a41e6681d56cba5025428.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find  ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty $, the graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=0.$ \n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches the value $0$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty $ as well. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
